WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.100 --> 00:10.520
- CHAIR YELLEN.

00:15.830 --> 00:28.190
- Good afternoon.

00:28.190 --> 00:35.470
- I am pleased to join you for the first of my post-FOMC press conferences.

00:35.470 --> 00:38.010
- Like Chairman Bernanke before me,

00:38.010 --> 00:42.600
- I appreciate the opportunity these press conferences afford

00:42.600 --> 00:48.410
- to explain the decisions of the FOMC and respond to your questions.

00:48.410 --> 00:54.830
- The Federal Open Market Committee concluded a two-day meeting earlier today.

00:54.830 --> 00:57.680
- As you already know from our statement,

00:57.680 --> 01:01.620
- the Committee decided to make another modest reduction in the pace

01:01.620 --> 01:06.050
- of its purchases of longer-term securities.

01:06.050 --> 01:10.810
- The Committee also updated its guidance regarding the likely future path

01:10.810 --> 01:13.570
- of the short-term interest rates.

01:13.570 --> 01:16.540
- As I'll explain more fully in a moment,

01:16.540 --> 01:20.980
- this change in our guidance does not indicate any change

01:20.980 --> 01:27.550
- in the Committee's policy intentions as set forth in its recent statements;

01:27.550 --> 01:33.400
- rather, the change is meant to clarify how the Committee anticipates policy

01:33.400 --> 01:39.710
- evolving after the unemployment rate declines below 6½ percent.

01:39.710 --> 01:45.720
- Let me explain the economic outlook that underlies these actions.

01:45.720 --> 01:48.850
- Despite some softer recent data,

01:48.850 --> 01:55.150
- the FOMC's outlook for continued progress toward our goals of maximum employment

01:55.150 --> 02:01.750
- and inflation returning to 2 percent remains broadly unchanged.

02:01.750 --> 02:04.300
- Unusually harsh weather in January

02:04.300 --> 02:08.540
- and February has made assessing the underlying strength

02:08.540 --> 02:13.520
- of the economy especially challenging.

02:13.520 --> 02:19.240
- Broadly speaking, however, the spending and production data,

02:19.240 --> 02:23.830
- while somewhat weaker than we had expected in January,

02:23.830 --> 02:29.060
- are roughly in line with our expectations as of December,

02:29.060 --> 02:34.580
- the last time Committee participants submitted economic projections.

02:34.580 --> 02:40.870
- In contrast, labor market conditions have continued to improve.

02:40.870 --> 02:44.590
- The unemployment rate, at 6.7 percent,

02:44.590 --> 02:47.740
- is three-tenths lower than the data available

02:47.740 --> 02:50.240
- at the time of the December meeting.

02:50.240 --> 02:56.350
- Further, broader measures of unemployment-such as the U-6 measure,

02:56.350 --> 03:01.710
- which includes marginally attached workers and those working part time

03:01.710 --> 03:06.490
- but preferring full-time work-have fallen even more

03:06.490 --> 03:10.200
- than the headline unemployment rate over this period,

03:10.200 --> 03:14.440
- and labor force participation has ticked up.

03:14.440 --> 03:17.450
- While the Committee continues to monitor developments

03:17.450 --> 03:20.210
- in global financial markets carefully,

03:20.210 --> 03:26.820
- financial conditions remain broadly consistent with the FOMC's objectives.

03:26.820 --> 03:34.360
- In sum, the FOMC continues to see sufficient underlying strength in the economy

03:34.360 --> 03:39.000
- to support ongoing improvement in the labor market.

03:39.000 --> 03:45.300
- Inflation has continued to run below the Committee's 2 percent objective.

03:45.300 --> 03:50.320
- Given that longer-term inflation expectations appear to be well anchored,

03:50.320 --> 03:55.740
- and in light of the ongoing recovery in the United States and in many economies

03:55.740 --> 04:00.730
- around the world, the FOMC continues to expect inflation

04:00.730 --> 04:04.670
- to move gradually back toward its objective.

04:04.670 --> 04:10.380
- The Committee is mindful that inflation running persistently below its objective

04:10.380 --> 04:14.770
- could pose risks to economic performance.

04:14.770 --> 04:17.500
- The Committee also recognizes, however,

04:17.500 --> 04:22.810
- that policy actions tend to exert pressure on inflation

04:22.810 --> 04:26.470
- that is manifest only gradually over time.

04:26.470 --> 04:33.510
- The FOMC will continue assessing incoming data carefully to ensure

04:33.510 --> 04:40.010
- that policy is consistent with attaining the FOMC's longer-run objectives

04:40.010 --> 04:44.470
- of maximum employment and inflation of 2 percent.

04:44.470 --> 04:49.890
- This outlook is reflected in the individual economic projections submitted

04:49.890 --> 04:57.150
- in conjunction with this meeting by the 16 FOMC participants-4 Board members

04:57.150 --> 05:00.310
- and 12 Reserve Bank presidents.

05:00.310 --> 05:08.390
- As always, each participant's projections are conditioned on his or her own view

05:08.390 --> 05:11.720
- of appropriate monetary policy.

05:11.720 --> 05:17.280
- The central tendency of the unemployment rate projections has shifted down by

05:17.280 --> 05:23.400
- about two-tenths since December and now stands at between 6.1

05:23.400 --> 05:28.180
- and 6.3 percent at the end of this year.

05:28.180 --> 05:30.040
- The unemployment rate is projected

05:30.040 --> 05:34.230
- to reach its longer-run normal level by the end of 2016.

05:34.230 --> 05:41.760
- The central tendency of the projections for real GDP growth stands at 2.8

05:41.760 --> 05:49.450
- to 3 percent for 2014 and remains somewhat above that of the estimates

05:49.450 --> 05:53.760
- of longer-run normal growth through 2016.

05:53.760 --> 05:58.110
- Meanwhile, as I noted, FOMC participants continue

05:58.110 --> 06:03.700
- to see inflation moving only gradually back toward 2 percent

06:03.700 --> 06:07.280
- over time as the economy expands.

06:07.280 --> 06:15.840
- The central tendency of the inflation projections is 1.5 to 1.6 percent in 2014,

06:15.840 --> 06:21.130
- rising to 1.7 to 2.0 percent in 2016.

06:21.130 --> 06:26.580
- Let me now return to our decision to make another measured reduction

06:26.580 --> 06:29.440
- in the pace of asset purchases.

06:29.440 --> 06:36.240
- Starting next month, we will be purchasing $55 billion of securities per month,

06:36.240 --> 06:40.870
- down $10 billion per month from our current rate.

06:40.870 --> 06:43.620
- Even after today's action takes effect,

06:43.620 --> 06:49.310
- we will continue to significantly expand our holdings of longer-term securities,

06:49.310 --> 06:54.960
- and we will also continue to roll over maturing Treasury securities

06:54.960 --> 07:00.710
- and reinvest principal payments from the FOMC's holdings of agency debt

07:00.710 --> 07:07.150
- and agency mortgage-backed securities in agency mortgage-backed securities.

07:07.150 --> 07:11.240
- These sizable and still-increasing holdings will continue

07:11.240 --> 07:14.900
- to put downward pressure on longer-term interest rates,

07:14.900 --> 07:21.400
- support mortgage markets, and make financial conditions more accommodative,

07:21.400 --> 07:25.470
- helping to support job creation and a return of inflation

07:25.470 --> 07:28.270
- to the Committee's objective.

07:28.270 --> 07:34.310
- The FOMC views today's decision to reduce the pace of asset purchases

07:34.310 --> 07:38.360
- as consistent with the decisionmaking framework laid

07:38.360 --> 07:43.590
- out last December and still in place today.

07:43.590 --> 07:51.090
- As before, if incoming information broadly supports the Committee's expectation

07:51.090 --> 07:55.390
- of ongoing improvement in labor markets and inflation moving back

07:55.390 --> 08:00.560
- over time toward its longer-run objective, the Committee will likely continue

08:00.560 --> 08:06.630
- to reduce the pace of asset purchases in measured steps at future meetings.

08:06.630 --> 08:09.940
- However, purchases are not on a preset course,

08:09.940 --> 08:14.940
- and the Committee's decisions about the pace of purchases remain contingent

08:14.940 --> 08:19.780
- on its outlook for jobs and inflation as well as its assessment

08:19.780 --> 08:24.390
- of the likely efficacy and costs of such purchases.

08:24.390 --> 08:29.520
- Today the FOMC also updated its forward guidance regarding the path

08:29.520 --> 08:32.210
- of short-term interest rates.

08:32.210 --> 08:37.950
- As emphasized in the statement, the new guidance does not indicate any change

08:37.950 --> 08:41.600
- in the policy intentions of the FOMC

08:41.600 --> 08:46.380
- but instead reflects changes in the conditions we face.

08:46.380 --> 08:49.610
- Let me explain this more fully.

08:49.610 --> 08:54.580
- In December 2012, the Committee first stated its guidance in terms

08:54.580 --> 08:59.610
- of economic thresholds, stipulating that the current low range

08:59.610 --> 09:04.850
- for the federal funds rate target would be appropriate at least as long

09:04.850 --> 09:09.540
- as the unemployment rate remains above 6½ percent,

09:09.540 --> 09:13.900
- inflation is projected to be no more than a half percentage point

09:13.900 --> 09:20.270
- above our longer-run goal, and longer-term inflation expectations remain

09:20.270 --> 09:22.100
- well anchored.

09:22.100 --> 09:26.620
- Since that time, progress in the labor market has been more rapid

09:26.620 --> 09:31.080
- than we had anticipated, while inflation has been lower

09:31.080 --> 09:34.260
- than the Committee had expected.

09:34.260 --> 09:38.550
- Although the thresholds served well as a useful guide to policy

09:38.550 --> 09:45.040
- over the past year, last December the FOMC judged it appropriate to update

09:45.040 --> 09:49.080
- that guidance, noting that the current target range

09:49.080 --> 09:54.590
- for the federal funds rate would likely be maintained "well past the time

09:54.590 --> 09:59.320
- that the unemployment rate declines below 6½ percent,

09:59.320 --> 10:02.130
- especially if projected inflation continues

10:02.130 --> 10:07.310
- to run below the Committee's 2 percent longer-run goal."

10:07.310 --> 10:12.400
- Today the Committee has further revised its forward guidance

10:12.400 --> 10:16.800
- to better reflect conditions as they now stand and are likely

10:16.800 --> 10:20.200
- to evolve over coming quarters.

10:20.200 --> 10:25.150
- The revised formulation starts with a general description of the factors

10:25.150 --> 10:33.440
- that drive FOMC decisionmaking and then provides the FOMC's current assessment

10:33.440 --> 10:38.060
- of what those factors will likely imply for the future path

10:38.060 --> 10:40.130
- of short-term interest rates.

10:40.130 --> 10:45.330
- In particular, the Committee states that "[i]n determining how long

10:45.330 --> 10:51.770
- to maintain the current 0 to ¼ percent target range for the federal funds rate,

10:51.770 --> 10:55.110
- it [the Committee] will assess progress-both realized

10:55.110 --> 10:57.990
- and expected-toward its objectives

10:57.990 --> 11:02.390
- of maximum employment and 2 percent inflation."

11:02.390 --> 11:06.450
- In short, the larger the shortfall of employment

11:06.450 --> 11:11.280
- or inflation from the respective objectives set by the FOMC,

11:11.280 --> 11:18.180
- and the longer any such shortfall is expected to persist,

11:18.180 --> 11:22.540
- the longer the target federal funds rate is likely to remain

11:22.540 --> 11:26.230
- in the present 0 to ¼ percent range.

11:26.230 --> 11:32.740
- The FOMC will base its ongoing assessment on a wide range of information,

11:32.740 --> 11:39.520
- including measures of labor market conditions, indicators of inflation pressures

11:39.520 --> 11:44.720
- and inflation expectations, and readings on financial developments.

11:44.720 --> 11:52.200
- As I've noted, the FOMC's assessment of these factors at present is consistent

11:52.200 --> 11:57.860
- with the characterization provided in previous forward guidance.

11:57.860 --> 12:00.420
- The Committee continues to anticipate

12:00.420 --> 12:05.040
- that conditions will likely warrant maintaining "the current range

12:05.040 --> 12:09.210
- for the federal funds rate for a considerable time

12:09.210 --> 12:13.440
- after the asset purchase program ends,

12:13.440 --> 12:16.880
- especially if projected inflation continues

12:16.880 --> 12:21.490
- to run below the Committee's 2 percent longer-run goal,

12:21.490 --> 12:26.150
- and provided that longer-term inflation expectations remain well anchored."

12:26.150 --> 12:32.250
- The FOMC also supplemented its guidance pertaining to the period

12:32.250 --> 12:37.060
- after the asset purchase program ends and the initial increase

12:37.060 --> 12:41.230
- in the federal funds rate target has occurred.

12:41.230 --> 12:45.700
- The statement continues to note that in deciding on the pace

12:45.700 --> 12:50.160
- for removing accommodation, the Committee will take a balanced approach

12:50.160 --> 12:52.960
- to attaining its objectives.

12:52.960 --> 12:58.650
- The statement now adds the Committee's current anticipation that "even

12:58.650 --> 13:04.470
- after employment and inflation are near mandate-consistent levels,

13:04.470 --> 13:09.070
- economic conditions may, for some time,

13:09.070 --> 13:14.140
- warrant keeping" short-term interest rates "below levels the Committee views

13:14.140 --> 13:17.590
- as normal in the longer run."

13:17.590 --> 13:23.190
- This guidance is consistent with the paths for appropriate policy as reported

13:23.190 --> 13:28.120
- in the participants' projections, which show the federal funds rate

13:28.120 --> 13:33.490
- for most participants remaining well below longer-run normal values

13:33.490 --> 13:36.690
- at the end of 2016.

13:36.690 --> 13:40.940
- Although FOMC participants provide a number of explanations

13:40.940 --> 13:47.120
- for the federal funds rate target remaining below its longer-run normal level,

13:47.120 --> 13:51.840
- many cite the residual impacts of the financial crisis,

13:51.840 --> 13:54.280
- and some note that the potential growth rate

13:54.280 --> 13:59.940
- of the economy may be lower, at least for a time.

13:59.940 --> 14:06.470
- In summary, the Committee's actions today reflect its assessment that progress

14:06.470 --> 14:11.730
- in the labor market is continuing, but that much remains to be done

14:11.730 --> 14:15.920
- on both the jobs and inflation fronts.

14:15.920 --> 14:19.070
- Unemployment is still elevated,

14:19.070 --> 14:24.620
- underemployment and long-term unemployment remain significant concerns,

14:24.620 --> 14:30.640
- and inflation is running significantly below the FOMC's objective.

14:30.640 --> 14:33.850
- These conditions warrant the continuation

14:33.850 --> 14:40.500
- of highly accommodative policy reflected in today's policy statement.

14:40.500 --> 14:44.560
- The Federal Reserve's interest rate guidance and its substantial

14:44.560 --> 14:50.220
- and still-increasing holdings of longer-term securities will ensure

14:50.220 --> 14:54.510
- that monetary policy remains highly accommodative,

14:54.510 --> 15:00.410
- promoting the FOMC's objectives of maximum employment and price stability.

15:00.410 --> 15:01.860
- Thank you.

15:01.860 --> 15:05.030
- I'll be glad to take your questions.

15:05.030 --> 15:13.250
- MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

15:13.250 --> 15:18.090
- Madam Chair, Marty Crutsinger with the Associated Press.

15:18.090 --> 15:21.850
- Could you give us a little insight in how the decision was made

15:21.850 --> 15:27.120
- on dropping the 6½ percent numerical target in the forward guidance?

15:27.120 --> 15:33.360
- Was there any concern expressed that there's been criticism on forward guidance,

15:33.360 --> 15:37.460
- that it's confusing markets, not helping them in some ways?

15:37.460 --> 15:41.400
- Was there concern expressed that perhaps it would have been better to go

15:41.400 --> 15:45.090
- to just a lower target, say, 6 percent?

15:45.090 --> 15:49.170
- And could you also address the concerns raised in the dissent

15:49.170 --> 15:55.770
- that by dropping this, it lowers the commitment on fighting low inflation?

15:55.770 --> 15:56.300
- Thank you.

15:56.300 --> 15:56.700
- CHAIR YELLEN.

15:56.700 --> 16:01.080
- Thanks. Well, as I mentioned in my statement,

16:01.080 --> 16:04.240
- the reason the Committee felt that the time had come

16:04.240 --> 16:07.340
- to revise the forward guidance is not

16:07.340 --> 16:10.310
- because we think it has not been effective.

16:10.310 --> 16:14.240
- I believe the Committee does think it's been effective.

16:14.240 --> 16:18.450
- I think it's had a very useful impact

16:18.450 --> 16:25.220
- in helping markets understand our expectations and shaping their own.

16:25.220 --> 16:30.740
- But it is becoming-as the unemployment rate gets closer

16:30.740 --> 16:37.190
- and closer to 6½ percent, to breaching that threshold that seems like the one

16:37.190 --> 16:39.720
- that is likely to be breached.

16:39.720 --> 16:45.010
- The question is: Markets want to know, the public wants to understand,

16:45.010 --> 16:49.340
- beyond that threshold, how will we decide what to do?

16:49.340 --> 16:55.400
- So the purpose of this change is simply to provide more information than we have

16:55.400 --> 17:00.220
- in the past, even though it is qualitative information,

17:00.220 --> 17:02.280
- about what we will be looking

17:02.280 --> 17:09.570
- at as the unemployment rate declines below 6½ percent in deciding how long

17:09.570 --> 17:14.580
- to hold the federal funds rate at this 0 to ¼ percent range.

17:14.580 --> 17:18.910
- And, as I said, we've tried to give a general formulation

17:18.910 --> 17:22.280
- of what we'll be looking at, which is: How far are we?

17:22.280 --> 17:26.330
- How large are the shortfalls in achieving our goals?

17:26.330 --> 17:29.230
- And how fast do we expect progress to be?

17:29.230 --> 17:33.910
- That will be the main factors we'll be looking at.

17:33.910 --> 17:39.520
- We initially started with an unemployment rate as a threshold.

17:39.520 --> 17:42.450
- That was easy enough for the Committee to say,

17:42.450 --> 17:46.400
- "With an unemployment rate above 6½ percent,

17:46.400 --> 17:49.380
- we know we're not close to full employment,

17:49.380 --> 17:54.050
- not close to an employment level consistent with our mandate,

17:54.050 --> 17:58.330
- and unless inflation were a significant concern,

17:58.330 --> 18:02.870
- we wouldn't dream of raising the federal funds rate target."

18:02.870 --> 18:05.950
- Now, the Committee has never felt

18:05.950 --> 18:10.750
- that the unemployment rate is a sufficient statistic for the labor market.

18:10.750 --> 18:14.520
- I think if I had to choose one indicator of the labor market,

18:14.520 --> 18:19.330
- the unemployment rate is probably as good a one as I could find.

18:19.330 --> 18:25.360
- But in assessing the real state of slack in the labor market and ultimately

18:25.360 --> 18:30.640
- of inflationary pressures that might-or deflationary pressures that could result

18:30.640 --> 18:34.490
- from that-it's appropriate to look at many more things.

18:34.490 --> 18:37.940
- And that's why the Committee now states we will look

18:37.940 --> 18:40.200
- at a broad range of information.

18:40.200 --> 18:45.960
- So the closer we get as we narrow in on coming closer to the target we want

18:45.960 --> 18:51.710
- to achieve, we will be carefully considering many indicators

18:51.710 --> 18:55.820
- of how close are we to our targets.

18:55.820 --> 18:58.160
- So those are the main reasons.

18:58.160 --> 19:02.680
- Now, you asked as well about the dissent.

19:02.680 --> 19:09.960
- President Kocherlakota felt-I believe he noted in his dissent

19:09.960 --> 19:15.750
- that he endorses the new guidance about the likely path

19:15.750 --> 19:23.630
- of the federal funds rate after we begin to finally raise it.

19:23.630 --> 19:29.820
- And that indicates that it's unlikely to be back to normal levels for some time,

19:29.820 --> 19:35.640
- but he questions whether or not the reformulated forward guidance shows

19:35.640 --> 19:41.270
- sufficient commitment of the Committee to its 2 percent inflation objective.

19:41.270 --> 19:46.780
- And I will simply say on my own behalf, and on behalf of the Committee,

19:46.780 --> 19:51.810
- that we are fully committed to the 2 percent inflation objective,

19:51.810 --> 19:58.340
- and we do not want to undershoot inflation for a prolonged period of time.

19:58.340 --> 20:02.850
- As I mentioned, monetary policy operates with lags, so,

20:02.850 --> 20:05.570
- the policies we have in place, we think,

20:05.570 --> 20:10.740
- will gradually-only gradually-move inflation back to 2 percent.

20:10.740 --> 20:16.560
- But if the Committee had real concerns that inflation were going

20:16.560 --> 20:19.790
- to remain persistently below 2 percent,

20:19.790 --> 20:23.160
- I feel confident that the Committee would act to prevent that.

20:23.160 --> 20:27.270
- JON HILSENRATH.

20:27.270 --> 20:29.950
- Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

20:29.950 --> 20:35.020
- Chair Yellen, in the interest rate projections made by FOMC participants

20:35.020 --> 20:39.410
- that supplement your statement, there seems to be a slight upward drift

20:39.410 --> 20:43.280
- in the expectations for rates going out to 2016.

20:43.280 --> 20:47.640
- For instance, a majority of officials see rates at 1 percent

20:47.640 --> 20:49.460
- or higher in this forecast round.

20:49.460 --> 20:54.930
- In the last forecast round, a majority saw officials less than 1 percent.

20:54.930 --> 20:59.740
- I wonder if you could explain why there is this small upward drift

20:59.740 --> 21:04.270
- in expected rates among Committee members,

21:04.270 --> 21:07.920
- whether these projections are a good guide for the public

21:07.920 --> 21:12.050
- about where rates-about the path of rates going forward,

21:12.050 --> 21:17.200
- and also how you reconcile this upward drift with the assurances

21:17.200 --> 21:19.070
- that the Committee makes in its statement

21:19.070 --> 21:23.610
- that rates will stay below normal levels well into the future.

21:23.610 --> 21:24.260
- CHAIR YELLEN.

21:24.260 --> 21:28.950
- Well, to my mind, there is only very limited upward drift.

21:28.950 --> 21:35.200
- You know, the Committee-I think the Committee,

21:35.200 --> 21:41.410
- in assessing the economy-if you compare today's assessment

21:41.410 --> 21:45.410
- with December's, is virtually identical.

21:45.410 --> 21:52.500
- Almost nothing has changed in the overall Committee assessment of the outlook.

21:52.500 --> 21:56.230
- As I mentioned, unemployment has come down.

21:56.230 --> 21:58.540
- The labor market more broadly, I think,

21:58.540 --> 22:02.690
- has improved a little more than we might have expected,

22:02.690 --> 22:06.280
- and that slightly more rapid improvement

22:06.280 --> 22:11.830
- in the unemployment picture might explain-I can't speak for why people write

22:11.830 --> 22:17.220
- down what they do, but-a little bit of the upward shift in those dots.

22:17.220 --> 22:23.120
- But, more generally, I think that one should not look to the dot plot,

22:23.120 --> 22:30.470
- so to speak, as the primary way in which the Committee wants to or is speaking

22:30.470 --> 22:34.170
- about policy to the public at large.

22:34.170 --> 22:41.460
- The FOMC statement is the device that the Committee as a policymaking group uses

22:41.460 --> 22:47.840
- to express its opinions, and we have expressed a number of opinions

22:47.840 --> 22:50.200
- about the likely path of rates.

22:50.200 --> 22:53.580
- In particular, the Committee has endorsed the view

22:53.580 --> 22:58.610
- that it anticipates it will be a considerable period

22:58.610 --> 23:03.590
- after the asset purchase program ends before it will be appropriate

23:03.590 --> 23:05.410
- to begin to raise rates.

23:05.410 --> 23:10.670
- And, of course, on our present path, well, that's not utterly preset.

23:10.670 --> 23:14.700
- We would be looking at next fall.

23:14.700 --> 23:17.590
- So, I think that's important guidance.

23:17.590 --> 23:23.910
- Looking further out, let's say if you look toward the end of 2016

23:23.910 --> 23:27.080
- when most participants are projecting

23:27.080 --> 23:33.630
- that the employment situation-that the unemployment rate will be close

23:33.630 --> 23:39.810
- to their notions of mandate-consistent or longer-run normal levels.

23:39.810 --> 23:46.580
- What you see, I think, if you look this time-if you gaze at the picture

23:46.580 --> 23:50.150
- from December or September, which is the first year

23:50.150 --> 23:55.210
- that we showed those dot plots for the end of 2016,

23:55.210 --> 24:02.310
- is the massive points that are notably below what the participants believe is

24:02.310 --> 24:07.790
- the normal longer-run level for nominal short-term rates,

24:07.790 --> 24:14.130
- and the Committee today, for the first time, endorsed that as a Committee view.

24:14.130 --> 24:16.460
- So I think that's significant.

24:16.460 --> 24:19.970
- I think that's what we should be paying attention to,

24:19.970 --> 24:26.320
- and I would simply warn you that these dots are going to move up

24:26.320 --> 24:29.680
- and down over time a little bit this way or that.

24:29.680 --> 24:34.300
- The dots moved down a little bit in December relative to September,

24:34.300 --> 24:37.520
- and they moved up ever so slightly.

24:37.520 --> 24:41.450
- I really don't think it's appropriate to read very much into it.

24:41.450 --> 24:45.590
- More generally, you know, the end of 2016 is a long way out.

24:45.590 --> 24:51.350
- Monetary policy will be geared to evolving conditions in the economy,

24:51.350 --> 24:56.960
- and the public does need to understand that as those views evolve,

24:56.960 --> 25:02.690
- the Committee's views on policy will likely evolve with them.

25:02.690 --> 25:07.980
- And that's a kind of uncertainty that the Committee wouldn't want

25:07.980 --> 25:13.530
- to eliminate completely from its guidance because we want the policy we put

25:13.530 --> 25:17.900
- in place to be appropriate to the economic conditions

25:17.900 --> 25:21.570
- that will prevail years down the road.

25:21.570 --> 25:24.200
- STEVE LIESMAN.

25:24.200 --> 25:24.430
- Thank you.

25:24.430 --> 25:25.440
- Steve Liesman, CNBC.

25:25.440 --> 25:28.060
- I wish to look not to the dots, but to the statement, as you suggest,

25:28.060 --> 25:32.020
- Madam Chair, and that one particular paragraph,

25:32.020 --> 25:36.190
- which says that the Committee anticipates a lower-than-normal rate even once you

25:36.190 --> 25:37.780
- return to the long run.

25:37.780 --> 25:40.070
- So, just so I understand correctly,

25:40.070 --> 25:42.980
- it means that once you hit the longer-run unemployment rate

25:42.980 --> 25:44.690
- of 5-which is the central tendency average

25:44.690 --> 25:48.500
- of being 5.4 percent-once you get a 2 percent inflation rate,

25:48.500 --> 25:54.590
- the market should not, then, anticipate the longer-run 4 percent fed funds rate,

25:54.590 --> 25:56.360
- and that would be question one.

25:56.360 --> 26:01.850
- Question two is, doesn't that implicitly suggest a shallower glide path once you

26:01.850 --> 26:05.660
- take off, or once fed funds rates would begin-when you first hike them?

26:05.660 --> 26:08.020
- Wouldn't that suggest a shallower glide path to the funds rate?

26:08.020 --> 26:08.630
- CHAIR YELLEN.

26:08.630 --> 26:11.960
- Yes, I think it does suggest a shallower glide path,

26:11.960 --> 26:18.600
- and what the Committee is expressing here, I would say,

26:18.600 --> 26:24.630
- is its forecast of what will be appropriate some years from now based

26:24.630 --> 26:31.480
- on its-the understanding that we've developed about what are the economic forces

26:31.480 --> 26:35.080
- that have been driving economic activity.

26:35.080 --> 26:41.660
- We've had a series of years now in which growth has proven disappointing.

26:41.660 --> 26:47.290
- Now, members of the Committee have different views about why this is likely

26:47.290 --> 26:52.830
- to be true, that the funds rate-when the labor market is normalized

26:52.830 --> 26:59.980
- and inflation is back to our objective-they maybe have slightly different views

26:59.980 --> 27:03.870
- on exactly why it's likely to be the case

27:03.870 --> 27:09.270
- that interest rates will be a little lower than they would in the longer run.

27:09.270 --> 27:13.810
- But for many, it's a matter of headwinds from the crisis

27:13.810 --> 27:17.970
- that have taken a very long time to dissipate and are likely

27:17.970 --> 27:20.910
- to continue being operative.

27:20.910 --> 27:23.980
- So, some examples I would say is,

27:23.980 --> 27:28.970
- we have under-many households are undergoing balance sheet repair.

27:28.970 --> 27:35.350
- There are many underwater mortgage holders,

27:35.350 --> 27:40.610
- difficulties therefore in gaining access to credit-for example,

27:40.610 --> 27:43.180
- through home equity lines of credit.

27:43.180 --> 27:48.830
- For some, that makes it difficult to finance small businesses.

27:48.830 --> 27:52.820
- Mortgage credit is very difficult for those still to get

27:52.820 --> 27:55.720
- without pristine credit scores.

27:55.720 --> 28:00.420
- That's improved somewhat over time, but it's not back to normal.

28:00.420 --> 28:07.370
- For some, fiscal policy is somewhat tighter than would be expected

28:07.370 --> 28:09.470
- over the next several years.

28:09.470 --> 28:16.840
- For some, it's headwinds from the global economy play a role as well.

28:16.840 --> 28:21.580
- But the general assessment is that even

28:21.580 --> 28:26.530
- after we've had an accommodative monetary policy for long enough

28:26.530 --> 28:32.750
- to get the economy back on track in the sense of meeting our objectives,

28:32.750 --> 28:38.590
- the stance of policy that will be appropriate to accomplish that will be easier

28:38.590 --> 28:43.600
- or involve somewhat lower than would be normal short-term interest rates.

28:43.600 --> 28:47.890
- Now, eventually, years later, most people think they will go back up.

28:47.890 --> 28:51.930
- But, as you said, that suggests the path will be gradual.

28:51.930 --> 28:55.220
- But I do want to emphasize, this is a forecast,

28:55.220 --> 28:59.950
- and this is the Committee's forecast based on its understanding

28:59.950 --> 29:03.160
- of the economy at this time.

29:03.160 --> 29:10.570
- And as we watch the economy over the next several years, that could evolve.

29:10.570 --> 29:18.430
- YLAN MUI. Hi, Ylan from the Washington Post.

29:18.430 --> 29:22.160
- You mentioned in your testimony on Capitol Hill recently that the Fed was trying

29:22.160 --> 29:26.730
- to assess the balance of weather effects versus more fundamental weakness

29:26.730 --> 29:31.050
- in the economy as the reason for the slowdown in growth in the first quarter,

29:31.050 --> 29:34.730
- and you guys mentioned in the statement weather specifically.

29:34.730 --> 29:38.010
- Does that mean that the Fed's analysis has come down on the side of weather,

29:38.010 --> 29:41.700
- or are you still concerned that there could be something else going

29:41.700 --> 29:43.140
- on that could be contributing to slower growth?

29:43.140 --> 29:46.850
- And you guys also lowered your forecast for GDP growth this year.

29:46.850 --> 29:48.000
- CHAIR YELLEN.

29:48.000 --> 29:54.410
- So, I'd say, certainly the analysis that we've done-and we did spend a lot

29:54.410 --> 29:58.910
- of time discussing weather and how it's affected businesses and households

29:58.910 --> 30:05.720
- in various parts of the country-certainly weather has played an important role

30:05.720 --> 30:08.740
- in weakening economic activity in Q1.

30:08.740 --> 30:17.520
- It's not the only factor that is at work, and most projections for growth

30:17.520 --> 30:20.160
- in the first quarter are reasonably weak.

30:20.160 --> 30:21.730
- It's an important factor.

30:21.730 --> 30:23.790
- It's not the only factor.

30:23.790 --> 30:30.500
- But I would say it's likely in the view of most of the Committee to begin

30:30.500 --> 30:35.320
- to wash out in the second quarter, and we can even see some rebound.

30:35.320 --> 30:37.640
- Now, I would say, I know what we've said

30:37.640 --> 30:41.570
- about weather is a little bit complicated and confusing.

30:41.570 --> 30:46.790
- So, you know, let me just say between December and January,

30:46.790 --> 30:52.530
- the Committee saw data that led it to be quite a bit more optimistic

30:52.530 --> 30:54.970
- about the economic outlook.

30:54.970 --> 31:01.390
- So I would say incoming data since January,

31:01.390 --> 31:09.140
- when our statement sounded quite an optimistic tone, partly down due to weather

31:09.140 --> 31:16.870
- and partly down because we probably overdid the optimism in January.

31:16.870 --> 31:20.740
- So, in some sense, our views have moved around here a little bit,

31:20.740 --> 31:28.240
- but if we take December to March, the Committee's views are largely unchanged.

31:29.470 --> 31:32.290
- JIM PUZZANGHERA.

31:36.870 --> 31:39.800
- Hi, Madam Chairwoman.

31:39.800 --> 31:42.440
- I'm Jim Puzzanghera with the L.A. Times.

31:42.440 --> 31:45.070
- You've been on-you've served on the Fed previously.

31:45.070 --> 31:49.850
- I'm wondering, now, in the past few weeks as Chairwoman,

31:49.850 --> 31:54.010
- what's been different about being on the Fed in your responsibilities as Chair,

31:54.010 --> 31:57.350
- compared to being just a Board of Governors member?

31:57.350 --> 31:57.790
- CHAIR YELLEN.

31:57.790 --> 31:58.800
- Well, thanks.

31:58.800 --> 32:03.830
- I feel I'm very lucky that I've had a lot of Fed experience to draw

32:03.830 --> 32:08.230
- on as I approach this role because it's complicated,

32:08.230 --> 32:15.280
- and now in many ways I feel the buck stops with me in terms of management

32:15.280 --> 32:21.970
- of the FOMC and responsibility to assure that the Federal Reserve makes progress

32:21.970 --> 32:27.970
- on its goals of getting the economy back on track and making progress

32:27.970 --> 32:33.090
- on our financial stability and regulation objectives.

32:33.090 --> 32:38.610
- So I feel that weight of responsibility keenly in the new role I have,

32:38.610 --> 32:42.150
- and I'm very committed to making sure

32:42.150 --> 32:47.650
- that I provide the leadership that's necessary for the Federal Reserve System

32:47.650 --> 32:50.180
- to move forward on these goals.

32:50.180 --> 32:55.710
- But, you know, in terms of the conduct of business,

32:55.710 --> 32:58.280
- it's pretty much the same as usual.

32:58.280 --> 33:01.710
- I'm not envisioning, nor have there been so far,

33:01.710 --> 33:06.410
- any radical changes in how the Federal Reserve does its business,

33:06.410 --> 33:10.380
- and that includes operating the FOMC.

33:10.380 --> 33:14.840
- ROBIN HARDING.

33:14.840 --> 33:17.480
- Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

33:17.480 --> 33:21.490
- Madam Chair, given that the new qualitative guidance doesn't give any

33:21.490 --> 33:25.480
- information about how you will trade off your unemployment

33:25.480 --> 33:29.340
- and inflation objectives, does it actually give us any information?

33:29.340 --> 33:34.580
- How will you trade off the risk of higher inflation versus faster progress

33:34.580 --> 33:37.600
- on unemployment as you get closer to full employment?

33:37.600 --> 33:37.900
- Thank you.

33:37.900 --> 33:39.050
- CHAIR YELLEN.

33:39.050 --> 33:43.110
- So, I'd say so far we haven't had that tradeoff to make

33:43.110 --> 33:48.000
- because inflation is running well below our objective,

33:48.000 --> 33:55.330
- and by any measure there remains substantial slack in the labor market.

33:55.330 --> 34:00.070
- So tradeoffs and worrying about doing more

34:00.070 --> 34:03.600
- or less because we have conflicting objectives,

34:03.600 --> 34:09.280
- this really has not been an element in our discussions about how

34:09.280 --> 34:12.380
- to be conducting policy now.

34:12.380 --> 34:18.730
- As we get closer to meeting our goals, it could become an element,

34:18.730 --> 34:22.520
- and I would say that we've given guidance

34:22.520 --> 34:26.200
- in the statement-and we gave perhaps more guidance

34:26.200 --> 34:31.800
- in our so-called consensus statement or statement of longer-run goals

34:31.800 --> 34:38.250
- and monetary policy strategies that we've now reaffirmed for three years

34:38.250 --> 34:43.240
- in a row-that the Committee would take a balanced approach in situations

34:43.240 --> 34:48.160
- where our objectives conflict and we're faced with tradeoffs

34:48.160 --> 34:52.000
- between inflation and unemployment.

34:52.000 --> 34:58.760
- When we first put our thresholds into effect, we envisioned a possible situation

34:58.760 --> 35:03.640
- where such a tradeoff could arise, where we might face a situation

35:03.640 --> 35:06.380
- where unemployment was quite high-namely,

35:06.380 --> 35:12.290
- over 6½ percent-and inflation might be drifting close to 2

35:12.290 --> 35:14.680
- or even a little bit above 2.

35:14.680 --> 35:20.880
- And our threshold-based guidance gave some more concrete indication

35:20.880 --> 35:26.260
- that we would tolerate inflation running a little bit over 2 percent

35:26.260 --> 35:31.610
- with unemployment sufficiently high before moving the federal funds rate

35:31.610 --> 35:32.930
- off zero.

35:32.930 --> 35:37.370
- And to the extent that that concrete guidance is useful,

35:37.370 --> 35:42.740
- the-I don't believe that's a situation that we're likely-if I thought

35:42.740 --> 35:48.730
- that that was a situation we were likely to encounter in the next several years,

35:48.730 --> 35:54.210
- we probably would have revised our forward guidance in a different way.

35:54.210 --> 35:57.930
- We revised it as we did, eliminating that language,

35:57.930 --> 36:02.520
- because it doesn't seem like a situation that's at all likely.

36:02.520 --> 36:08.820
- But I would point you to the statement-the final statement in this statement

36:08.820 --> 36:16.030
- that says that the FOMC does not see this guidance as indicating any change

36:16.030 --> 36:23.160
- in our policy intentions, and I would include how we would make tradeoffs

36:23.160 --> 36:26.990
- between our inflation and employment objectives

36:26.990 --> 36:29.490
- if we were to face that situation.

36:29.490 --> 36:32.900
- ANN SAPHIR.

36:37.190 --> 36:39.490
- Ann Saphir with Reuters.

36:39.490 --> 36:44.110
- First, I just wanted a quick clarification.

36:44.110 --> 36:48.840
- You said that something would happen by next fall,

36:48.840 --> 36:52.320
- and we-on a clear path-on a path until next fall.

36:52.320 --> 36:55.260
- I was unclear if you were speaking of rate hikes

36:55.260 --> 36:57.410
- or if you were speaking- CHAIR YELLEN.

36:57.410 --> 37:04.250
- I simply meant to say that if we continued to reduce the pace

37:04.250 --> 37:09.560
- of our asset purchases in the manner that we have, in measured steps,

37:09.560 --> 37:13.330
- that the program would be winding down next fall.

37:13.330 --> 37:13.660
- ANN SAPHIR.

37:13.660 --> 37:16.470
- In this coming fall, you mean, not the fall of next year,

37:16.470 --> 37:18.100
- is that-just- CHAIR YELLEN.

37:18.100 --> 37:18.860
- Yes, this coming fall.

37:18.860 --> 37:19.040
- ANN SAPHIR.

37:19.040 --> 37:21.370
- To be clear-I just wanted to be clear about that.

37:21.370 --> 37:25.980
- Then once you do wind down the bond buying program,

37:25.980 --> 37:31.090
- could you tell us how long of a gap we might expect before the rate hikes

37:31.090 --> 37:31.560
- do begin?

37:31.560 --> 37:33.070
- CHAIR YELLEN.

37:33.070 --> 37:40.010
- So, the language that we use in the statement is "considerable" period.

37:40.010 --> 37:46.460
- So, I-you know, this is the kind of term-it's hard to define.

37:46.460 --> 37:52.880
- But, you know, it probably means something on the order

37:52.880 --> 37:56.290
- of around six months or that type of thing.

37:56.290 --> 37:58.270
- But, you know, it depends.

37:58.270 --> 38:04.010
- What the statement is saying is, it depends what conditions are like.

38:04.010 --> 38:07.500
- We need to see where the labor market is,

38:07.500 --> 38:14.340
- how close are we to our full employment goal-that will be a complicated

38:14.340 --> 38:19.910
- assessment not just based on a single statistic-and how rapidly are we moving

38:19.910 --> 38:20.280
- toward it?

38:20.280 --> 38:23.060
- Are we really close and moving fast?

38:23.060 --> 38:26.870
- Or are we getting closer but moving very slowly?

38:26.870 --> 38:32.800
- And then, what the statement emphasizes, and this is the same language we used

38:32.800 --> 38:36.870
- in December and January, we used the language especially

38:36.870 --> 38:40.610
- if inflation is running below our 2 percent objective.

38:40.610 --> 38:42.530
- Inflation matters here, too,

38:42.530 --> 38:47.060
- and our general principle tries to capture that notion.

38:47.060 --> 38:50.730
- If we have a substantial shortfall in inflation,

38:50.730 --> 38:56.490
- if inflation is persistently running below our 2 percent objective,

38:56.490 --> 38:59.530
- that is a very good reason to hold the funds rate

38:59.530 --> 39:02.110
- at its present range for longer.

39:03.190 --> 39:05.090
- BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

39:05.090 --> 39:09.720
- The Committee's Vice Chairman, Bill Dudley, said recently,

39:09.720 --> 39:12.290
- "If the economy decided it was going to grow at 5 percent

39:12.290 --> 39:14.890
- or the economy decided it wasn't going to grow at all,

39:14.890 --> 39:16.890
- those would be the kind of changes in the outlook

39:16.890 --> 39:19.710
- that I think would warrant changing the pace of taper[ing]."

39:19.710 --> 39:22.580
- Is that an accurate description of what you mean when you say that you're not

39:22.580 --> 39:25.330
- on a preset course, anything between zero to five?

39:25.330 --> 39:28.510
- And, secondly, if I may, there's a lot of research showing

39:28.510 --> 39:32.460
- that short-term unemployment seems to be responsible for the level of inflation

39:32.460 --> 39:35.730
- and that long-term unemployment seems relatively uncorrelated.

39:35.730 --> 39:37.430
- Is that the Fed's view at this point?

39:37.430 --> 39:39.850
- Is that one reason that you expect inflation to rebound

39:39.850 --> 39:41.760
- in the next couple of years?

39:41.760 --> 39:42.390
- CHAIR YELLEN.

39:42.390 --> 39:48.110
- So I think the numbers you cited would be extremes in terms

39:48.110 --> 39:51.420
- of defining what we'd need to see.

39:51.420 --> 39:53.910
- I wouldn't go to such extremes.

39:53.910 --> 39:58.930
- I guess the way I would put it is this:

39:58.930 --> 40:04.420
- There are two conditions for the Committee to decide

40:04.420 --> 40:07.980
- to continue tapering the pace of purchases.

40:07.980 --> 40:16.770
- The first is that we need to assess that the labor market continues to be

40:16.770 --> 40:22.630
- on the mend and that we feel reasonably satisfied that the outlook is

40:22.630 --> 40:27.470
- for further improvement in the labor market that will get us back

40:27.470 --> 40:30.360
- to our maximum employment objective.

40:30.360 --> 40:33.760
- And, second of all, we need to see-and now coming back

40:33.760 --> 40:37.080
- to inflation again-inflation is low,

40:37.080 --> 40:39.950
- and it's been running well below our objective.

40:39.950 --> 40:46.310
- And we need to see evidence that leaves us feeling satisfied

40:46.310 --> 40:48.990
- that inflation will move up over time,

40:48.990 --> 40:54.680
- that we believe the evidence is consistent with its moving up over time.

40:54.680 --> 41:02.320
- If the Committee no longer feels comfortable making such assessments-so,

41:02.320 --> 41:07.350
- if there is enough change in the data we're seeing about the economy

41:07.350 --> 41:12.260
- that it no longer seems reasonable or convincing

41:12.260 --> 41:15.150
- to make those two separate assessments,

41:15.150 --> 41:19.880
- then a case has been made to change the pace of asset purchases

41:19.880 --> 41:22.120
- and to deviate from the current plan.

41:22.120 --> 41:26.250
- So 5 percent and 1 percent, those are very extreme numbers.

41:26.250 --> 41:30.980
- But I would want to feel confident in making those two statements

41:30.980 --> 41:34.810
- about the labor market and inflation.

41:34.810 --> 41:40.820
- With respect to the issue of short-term unemployment and its being more relevant

41:40.820 --> 41:44.910
- for inflation and a better measure of the labor market,

41:44.910 --> 41:47.640
- I've seen research along those lines.

41:47.640 --> 41:53.560
- I think it would be tremendously premature to adopt any notion that says

41:53.560 --> 41:58.340
- that that is an accurate read on either how inflation is determined

41:58.340 --> 42:01.240
- or what constitutes slack in the labor market.

42:01.240 --> 42:05.610
- So I think this is something our Committee will be looking at,

42:05.610 --> 42:08.760
- especially as unemployment goes down

42:08.760 --> 42:14.570
- and other labor market indicators hopefully simultaneously improve.

42:14.570 --> 42:17.670
- We'll be looking at a broad range of indicators.

42:17.670 --> 42:23.580
- We're looking to see progress on many different dimensions where we see slack

42:23.580 --> 42:27.090
- in the economy, but I wouldn't endorse,

42:27.090 --> 42:30.350
- and I certainly don't think our Committee would endorse,

42:30.350 --> 42:33.700
- the judgment of the research that you cited.

42:33.700 --> 42:36.280
- JEFF KEARNS.

42:39.670 --> 42:41.960
- Jeff Kearns from Bloomberg News.

42:41.960 --> 42:44.910
- You've spoken in the past about-thinking about back to March

42:44.910 --> 42:49.750
- of last year-of how you supplement your view

42:49.750 --> 42:54.650
- of the labor market beyond unemployment with other gauges like quit rates

42:54.650 --> 42:56.640
- and layoffs and things like that.

42:56.640 --> 43:04.070
- How has your dashboard evolved in the past few months in terms both

43:04.070 --> 43:11.950
- of the-which indicators you like to watch most, and also in terms of the quality

43:11.950 --> 43:14.340
- of data that you think-whether it's positive,

43:14.340 --> 43:17.570
- negative-that you're getting from these indicators.

43:17.570 --> 43:19.120
- Thank you.

43:19.120 --> 43:20.060
- CHAIR YELLEN.

43:20.060 --> 43:23.570
- So I have talked in the past about indicators I like to watch

43:23.570 --> 43:27.010
- or I think that are relevant in assessing the labor market.

43:27.010 --> 43:30.840
- In addition to the standard unemployment rate,

43:30.840 --> 43:34.090
- I certainly look at broader measures of unemployment.

43:34.090 --> 43:37.870
- I mentioned U-6 in my statement.

43:37.870 --> 43:44.580
- It-5 percent of the labor force working part time on an involuntary basis,

43:44.580 --> 43:50.760
- that's an exceptionally high number relative to the measured unemployment rate,

43:50.760 --> 43:57.350
- and it-so, to my mind, is a form of slack that is-adds to what we see

43:57.350 --> 44:02.250
- in the normal unemployment rate and is unusually large.

44:02.250 --> 44:05.460
- However, it is coming down, as well as U-3.

44:05.460 --> 44:11.160
- It's moving in the right direction and has moved even more recently than U-3.

44:11.160 --> 44:17.600
- Of course, I watch discouraged and marginally attached workers.

44:17.600 --> 44:23.360
- The share of long-term unemployment has been immensely high

44:23.360 --> 44:27.690
- and can be very stubborn in bringing down,

44:27.690 --> 44:30.230
- that's something that I watch closely.

44:30.230 --> 44:35.810
- Again, that remains exceptionally high, but it has come down from something

44:35.810 --> 44:42.770
- like 45 percent to high 30s, but that's certainly on my dashboard.

44:42.770 --> 44:49.990
- Labor force participation-I do think most research suggests that due

44:49.990 --> 44:55.540
- to demographic factors, labor force participation will be coming down,

44:55.540 --> 44:59.860
- and there has been a downward trend now for a number of years.

44:59.860 --> 45:04.990
- But I think there is also a cyclical component in the fact

45:04.990 --> 45:08.490
- that labor force participation is depressed.

45:08.490 --> 45:14.020
- And so, it may be that as the economy begins to strengthen,

45:14.020 --> 45:19.120
- we could see labor force participation flatten out for a time

45:19.120 --> 45:23.830
- as discouraged workers start moving back into the labor market.

45:23.830 --> 45:26.580
- And so that's something I'm watching closely.

45:26.580 --> 45:29.860
- In the Committee, we'll have to watch-there are different views on this

45:29.860 --> 45:34.890
- within the Committee, and it's hard to know definitively what part

45:34.890 --> 45:39.380
- of labor force participation is structural versus cyclical,

45:39.380 --> 45:42.260
- so it's something to watch closely.

45:42.260 --> 45:47.150
- I've also mentioned, in the past, measures of labor market turnover.

45:47.150 --> 45:50.030
- You mentioned quits.

45:50.030 --> 45:56.740
- A remarkably large share of workers quit their jobs every month,

45:56.740 --> 46:00.130
- usually going directly into another job.

46:00.130 --> 46:05.780
- And I take the quit rate in many ways as a sign of the health of the economy.

46:05.780 --> 46:10.640
- When workers are scared they won't be able to get other jobs,

46:10.640 --> 46:14.140
- they show a reduced willingness to quit their jobs.

46:14.140 --> 46:20.680
- Now, quit rates now are below normal pre-recession levels,

46:20.680 --> 46:23.940
- but on the other hand, they have come up over time,

46:23.940 --> 46:26.410
- and so we have seen improvement.

46:26.410 --> 46:31.330
- The job opening rate has also come up.

46:31.330 --> 46:35.170
- The hires rate, however, remains extremely depressed,

46:35.170 --> 46:39.500
- and I take that as a sign of a weaker labor market.

46:39.500 --> 46:44.860
- But most of these measures, although they don't paint the identical extent

46:44.860 --> 46:48.830
- of improvement, if you ask about my dashboard,

46:48.830 --> 46:54.200
- the dial on virtually all of those things is moving

46:54.200 --> 46:57.320
- in a direction of improvement.

46:57.320 --> 47:02.930
- The final thing I'd mention is wages, and wage growth has really been very low.

47:02.930 --> 47:08.160
- I know there is perhaps one isolated measure of wage growth

47:08.160 --> 47:12.820
- that suggests some uptick, but most measures

47:12.820 --> 47:16.780
- of wage increase are running at very low levels.

47:16.780 --> 47:23.000
- In fact, with the productivity growth we have and 2 percent inflation,

47:23.000 --> 47:27.550
- one would probably expect to see, on an ongoing basis,

47:27.550 --> 47:34.230
- something between-perhaps 3 and 4 percent wage inflation would be normal.

47:34.230 --> 47:37.110
- Wage inflation has been running at 2 percent.

47:37.110 --> 47:42.820
- So not only is it depressed, signaling weakness in the labor market,

47:42.820 --> 47:45.450
- but it is certainly not flashing.

47:45.450 --> 47:52.250
- An increase in it might signal some tightening or meaningful pressures

47:52.250 --> 47:55.700
- on inflation, at least over time.

47:55.700 --> 47:58.880
- And I would say we're not seeing that.

47:58.880 --> 48:01.760
- WYATT ANDREWS.

48:03.760 --> 48:05.650
- Madam Chair, Wyatt Andrews from CBS.

48:05.650 --> 48:10.800
- For tens of millions of Americans, the recovery is an awful long time coming.

48:10.800 --> 48:14.650
- May we know your thoughts on why the recovery is so slow,

48:14.650 --> 48:17.720
- and why the economy is not creating more jobs?

48:17.720 --> 48:19.090
- CHAIR YELLEN.

48:19.090 --> 48:22.270
- Well, I think the short answer is that we have lived

48:22.270 --> 48:28.140
- through a devastating financial crisis that has taken an exceptional toll

48:28.140 --> 48:36.110
- on the economy in many different ways, from housing to leaving a huge number

48:36.110 --> 48:44.950
- of homeowners with mortgages living in homes with mortgages that are underwater,

48:44.950 --> 48:51.330
- has had a highly negative effect on their credit ratings and their ability

48:51.330 --> 48:59.940
- to access credit, has left businesses with very cautious attitudes that we see

48:59.940 --> 49:04.990
- in business investment spending that is very restrained.

49:04.990 --> 49:09.250
- On top of that, we've had weakness in the global economy,

49:09.250 --> 49:12.660
- and we've had a very tight fiscal policy at home.

49:12.660 --> 49:19.500
- After stimulus at the onset of the recession,

49:19.500 --> 49:25.170
- we've had a good deal of fiscal consolidation in the United States and,

49:25.170 --> 49:33.460
- at a time when fiscal policy normally in the past would have been serving

49:33.460 --> 49:38.510
- to create jobs, fiscal policy from that standpoint has served

49:38.510 --> 49:40.380
- as a headwind to the recovery.

49:40.380 --> 49:42.980
- And at the federal-especially at the federal level,

49:42.980 --> 49:45.790
- but also with state and local levels as well.

49:45.790 --> 49:49.490
- And so we have had a disappointing recovery.

49:49.490 --> 49:55.240
- And monetary policy has tried to do what we can to offset that.

49:55.240 --> 50:00.210
- But, you know, the linkages aren't as strong and aren't as quick

50:00.210 --> 50:03.510
- as we might ideally like them to be.

50:03.510 --> 50:07.250
- GREG ROBB.

50:07.250 --> 50:08.340
- Thank you.

50:08.340 --> 50:11.290
- I'd like to take you back to last summer

50:11.290 --> 50:16.010
- when there were hints-the Fed made hints that they were going to taper,

50:16.010 --> 50:20.970
- and long-term interest rates spiked, mortgage rates rose.

50:20.970 --> 50:24.320
- What lessons-looking back at that crisis, at that period,

50:24.320 --> 50:26.540
- what lessons have you learned from it,

50:26.540 --> 50:31.980
- and are you confident that you won't repeat that-those mistakes again?

50:31.980 --> 50:32.750
- CHAIR YELLEN.

50:32.750 --> 50:39.060
- Well, I think there were quite a number of things happening at that time.

50:39.060 --> 50:46.440
- I think it's probably true that monetary policy may have played a role

50:46.440 --> 50:49.560
- in touching off that market reaction,

50:49.560 --> 50:54.320
- but I think the market reaction was exacerbated by the fact

50:54.320 --> 50:59.240
- that we had a very significant unwinding of carry trades

50:59.240 --> 51:04.190
- and other leveraged positions that investors had taken,

51:04.190 --> 51:11.240
- perhaps thinking that the level of volatility was exceptionally low

51:11.240 --> 51:15.910
- and perhaps lower than was safe for them to have assumed.

51:15.910 --> 51:20.120
- But we certainly saw-now, in some ways,

51:20.120 --> 51:25.430
- the fact that term premia in interest rates have come up somewhat,

51:25.430 --> 51:32.310
- although it has had a negative effect on the recovery and that's evident

51:32.310 --> 51:39.000
- in housing-in the slowdown in housing-perhaps it's diminished some financial

51:39.000 --> 51:45.140
- instability risk that may have been associated with these carry trades

51:45.140 --> 51:50.330
- and speculative activities that were unwinding during that time.

51:50.330 --> 51:56.270
- A lesson is that we will try, and we were trying then,

51:56.270 --> 52:03.750
- but we will continue to try to communicate as clearly as we possibly can

52:03.750 --> 52:10.800
- about how we will conduct monetary policy and to be as steady and determined

52:10.800 --> 52:16.720
- and as transparent as we can to provide as much clarity

52:16.720 --> 52:21.450
- as is reasonably certain-given that the economic developments

52:21.450 --> 52:26.860
- in the economy are themselves uncertain-but we will try as hard as we can not

52:26.860 --> 52:29.870
- to be a source of instability here.

52:31.090 --> 52:34.460
- REBECCA JARVIS.

52:35.920 --> 52:37.460
- Madam Chair, Rebecca Jarvis, ABC News.

52:37.460 --> 52:43.500
- One of the drivers last spring and summer of home prices and home sales was

52:43.500 --> 52:46.800
- that sense that interest rates were going up, that they were spiking.

52:46.800 --> 52:50.790
- And now, a year later, we're looking essentially at a flat interest rate picture

52:50.790 --> 52:53.020
- as far as homebuyers are concerned.

52:53.020 --> 52:57.070
- So, is there any sense on your Committee that staying

52:57.070 --> 53:00.840
- at this level loses its punch the longer we remain here?

53:00.840 --> 53:04.400
- And if I'm a buyer, and I'm thinking about going out and buying a home,

53:04.400 --> 53:07.980
- why should I do that today as opposed to waiting a few more years

53:07.980 --> 53:11.470
- or even months before interest rates then do go up?

53:11.470 --> 53:12.460
- CHAIR YELLEN.

53:12.460 --> 53:17.210
- Well, I think the level of interest rates remains low by historic levels.

53:17.210 --> 53:24.000
- And the level of household formation is very depressed,

53:24.000 --> 53:26.480
- has been very depressed for some time.

53:26.480 --> 53:30.740
- There are a lot of kids who were shacking up with their families

53:30.740 --> 53:37.870
- and probably would like to be going out and acquiring places of their own,

53:37.870 --> 53:41.220
- whether it's an apartment or a home.

53:41.220 --> 53:47.850
- There's a lot of demographic potential there for new household formation

53:47.850 --> 53:53.590
- that would ultimately generate new construction, either single or multifamily,

53:53.590 --> 53:56.370
- and the level of rates I think does matter.

53:56.370 --> 54:01.050
- And the fact that they're low now, I think,

54:01.050 --> 54:04.460
- is something that should serve as a stimulus

54:04.460 --> 54:08.190
- to people coming back into the housing market.

54:08.190 --> 54:15.190
- And what we've not yet seen-the pickup after the lull-after interest rates went

54:15.190 --> 54:19.550
- up last summer, I do expect housing activity to begin

54:19.550 --> 54:22.710
- to expand more rapidly later on.

54:22.710 --> 54:27.790
- I don't think it's only the expectation that "I have to move now

54:27.790 --> 54:34.690
- or things will be more expensive later" that spurs those decisions.

54:34.690 --> 54:39.300
- So- KATE DAVIDSON.

54:46.650 --> 54:49.090
- Hi, Kate Davidson from Politico.

54:49.090 --> 54:51.060
- Much has been made about the fact that you

54:51.060 --> 54:54.200
- and your predecessor agreed on many policies.

54:54.200 --> 54:56.170
- You shared a lot of the same policy views.

54:56.170 --> 54:59.760
- Can you tell us one way in which your chairmanship will be different

54:59.760 --> 55:01.580
- than Ben Bernanke's?

55:04.990 --> 55:06.840
- CHAIR YELLEN.

55:06.840 --> 55:11.500
- Well, I think we are committed to exactly the same set of goals.

55:11.500 --> 55:21.520
- And, you know, as I indicated, I-my goal, and I will, you know,

55:21.520 --> 55:28.420
- throw myself into this as wholeheartedly as I can, is to make rapid progress,

55:28.420 --> 55:34.110
- as rapid progress as we possibly can in getting this recovery back on track

55:34.110 --> 55:37.440
- and putting Americans back to work and into jobs,

55:37.440 --> 55:40.390
- and in moving inflation back up to levels

55:40.390 --> 55:45.020
- at the Committee's target of 2 percent.

55:45.020 --> 55:50.160
- My predecessor was also devoted to that.

55:50.160 --> 55:54.080
- Strengthening the financial system is a work in progress,

55:54.080 --> 56:01.200
- and he made large inroads in strengthening the financial system.

56:01.200 --> 56:06.890
- I'd just say, there is more work to be done, I have a long to-do list.

56:06.890 --> 56:13.230
- I would absolutely-it's high priority for me to see further work done

56:13.230 --> 56:16.190
- in addressing too-big-to-fail.

56:16.190 --> 56:19.910
- We have a to-do list of things we want to accomplish,

56:19.910 --> 56:26.030
- and in assessing threats to financial stability because neither one

56:26.030 --> 56:31.900
- of us-and no one wants to live through a financial crisis like the last one,

56:31.900 --> 56:35.550
- and we want to be extremely cognizant

56:35.550 --> 56:38.750
- of emerging threats to the financial system.

56:38.750 --> 56:42.870
- So I haven't answered your question by saying that I will be different,

56:42.870 --> 56:46.470
- but I think he had a very good agenda, and it's one I shared.

56:46.470 --> 56:49.950
- It's why I came to Washington to be Vice Chair,

56:49.950 --> 56:53.990
- and it's the agenda I expect to continue pursuing.

56:53.990 --> 56:55.000
- PETER BARNES.

56:55.000 --> 57:02.020
- Madam Chair, Peter Barnes of Fox Business.

57:02.020 --> 57:06.550
- I wanted to talk about international developments and the crisis in the Ukraine.

57:06.550 --> 57:10.100
- Is the crisis a headwind for the U.S. economy?

57:10.100 --> 57:12.950
- Are there risks to the U.S. economy

57:12.950 --> 57:18.050
- and the U.S. banking system, directly and indirectly?

57:18.050 --> 57:22.470
- And did the Russians move $100 billion in U.S. Treasury securities

57:22.470 --> 57:25.840
- out of the United States in the last couple of weeks to avoid U.S. sanctions?

57:25.840 --> 57:30.810
- Those are-those foreign securities are held by the Fed.

57:30.810 --> 57:31.710
- Thank you.

57:31.710 --> 57:32.310
- CHAIR YELLEN.

57:32.310 --> 57:35.410
- So let me start with the last piece of your question first.

57:35.410 --> 57:44.010
- I'm sorry, I-movements in custodial accounts at the New York Fed are something

57:44.010 --> 57:48.310
- that I can't-I'm not in the position to be able to comment about.

57:48.310 --> 57:52.100
- But in terms of the situation in Ukraine and Russia,

57:52.100 --> 57:56.140
- it's something that we're monitoring very closely.

57:56.140 --> 58:01.800
- We discussed in our meeting the direct-trade linkages

58:01.800 --> 58:09.910
- or exposures of the U.S. banking system to the Ukraine and Russia are not large.

58:09.910 --> 58:13.730
- That's-we're not seeing meaningful impacts now,

58:13.730 --> 58:20.560
- but obviously there are geopolitical risks here that it's very important for us

58:20.560 --> 58:24.040
- to be attentive to and to keep our eye on.

58:24.040 --> 58:30.150
- And we're not seeing broader global financial repercussions,

58:30.150 --> 58:34.660
- but if this were to escalate, that would certainly be something that would be

58:34.660 --> 58:38.320
- on our radar screen, but we're not seeing-we're not seeing that now

58:38.320 --> 58:40.690
- and we're monitoring it closely.

58:43.860 --> 58:44.860
- VICTORIA MCGRANE.

58:44.860 --> 58:48.510
- Victoria McGrane from Dow Jones Newswires.

58:48.510 --> 58:54.490
- You've spoken about how unemployment is more than just statistics to you.

58:54.490 --> 58:57.940
- And I wanted to ask, when you make that statement,

58:57.940 --> 59:02.020
- who do you have in mind or-and what do you do, if anything,

59:02.020 --> 59:07.300
- to keep in touch with that kind of human side of the impact

59:07.300 --> 59:11.130
- of the economic crisis and slow recovery that we've had?

59:11.130 --> 59:11.960
- CHAIR YELLEN.

59:11.960 --> 59:17.510
- Well, I'd be surprised if anyone in this room doesn't know someone

59:17.510 --> 59:21.480
- who has been touched by the crisis, by unemployment,

59:21.480 --> 59:27.920
- by difficulties in getting jobs, and that is true of me and my family

59:27.920 --> 59:33.600
- and friends, I think, as it is probably for many of you.

59:33.600 --> 59:40.890
- I talk to a broad range of business contacts and try to stay in touch

59:40.890 --> 59:45.050
- with what's happening with real people in the economy.

59:45.050 --> 59:50.830
- We have-do a lot of work in community development in the Fed

59:50.830 --> 59:53.640
- and have groups come to talk to us

59:53.640 --> 59:58.250
- and explain to us how their communities have been affected

59:58.250 --> 01:00:02.730
- by the economic situation and by the housing crisis.

01:00:02.730 --> 01:00:08.910
- When I was in San Francisco, we worked-you know, we had programs there,

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:14.060
- we worked very closely, particularly in low-income communities

01:00:14.060 --> 01:00:16.780
- that have been very badly affected,

01:00:16.780 --> 01:00:20.610
- to design programs that could potentially be helpful.

01:00:20.610 --> 01:00:27.670
- We tried to study what kinds of programs can be most effective

01:00:27.670 --> 01:00:34.180
- and to try to understand what kinds of advice we could give to those

01:00:34.180 --> 01:00:37.860
- in the community development lending field to help.

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:47.870
- So I do try to listen to people that represent communities

01:00:47.870 --> 01:00:50.880
- that are experiencing the worst of the crisis

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:56.340
- and stay in touch with it that way.

